  },
  "articleBody": "There's nothing more frustrating than getting a beautiful new window only to realize it doesn't quite fit the opening. This usually happens because of a mix-up with measurements or not understanding rough opening requirements. The rough opening needs to be precisely sized to your specific window unit, allowing for installation clearance and shimming. If it's too small, the window literally won't go in. If it's too big, you'll have excessive gaps that are hard to seal properly, leading to drafts and potential water intrusion. We often see folks confusing nominal window dimensions with the actual frame size, or only measuring once instead of checking the top, middle, and bottom, as well as both sides of the opening. These slight discrepancies can cause big problems during installation. An ill-fitting window can also compromise its performance and longevity, leading to issues with operation and even voiding warranties. It’s critical that the rough opening is perfectly plumb, level, and square. If you're tackling a window replacement, especially in an older home, or if you're creating a new opening in a wall that might be load-bearing, it's wise to bring in a professional. We can ensure accurate measurements and proper framing, saving you time and money in the long run. Don't let a mis-sized opening turn your new window into a headache!",
